I’ve found a general, low-blast-radius enforcement point: when `lint_and_remove_uninhabited` is about to remove the return edge for an uninhabited call, inspect the MIR call’s callee. If it is Verus’s erased ghost value shim, the “does not continue” branch is an artifact of erasure, so keep the edge. Real diverging calls and genuinely uninhabited returns keep Rust’s existing behavior.
